study("amanzio1999-expectation-antagonism", "amanzio1999-placebo-dissection", "Human experimental ischemic arm pain; unconditioned verbal-cue/naloxone groups",
  "Use tourniquet-induced ischemic arm pain and the participant's time to intolerable pain. Compare repeated no-treatment days, hidden naloxone, an openly administered saline injection described as an analgesic and an openly administered naloxone injection given the same analgesic description. Retain separate groups and pre/post no-treatment days; the 229 analyzed people across twelve groups are not 229 independent replications of each contrast.",
  "Pain-tolerance duration after verbal/injection and opioid-antagonist manipulations", "Analgesic-described saline increases tolerance; the analogous naloxone condition lacks that increase, while hidden naloxone alone has no significant tolerance effect.",
  ["No direct endogenous opioid concentration, release timing or anatomical route is measured.", "The stated complete blockade and hidden-drug null are source statistical interpretations, not independently demonstrated equivalence; expectation change itself was not quantified."], [0, 1, 3]);
observation("placebo-expectation-intervention", "Analgesic framing and opioid-antagonist comparison", "amanzio1999-expectation-antagonism", "The experimental input combines analgesic verbal framing with an open injection, against no-treatment baseline and a hidden-naloxone control. Naloxone is an active antagonist used to test the framing-associated response, not an inert placebo ingredient.");
observation("placebo-opioid-sensitive-tolerance", "Naloxone-sensitive pain-tolerance response", "amanzio1999-expectation-antagonism", "Analgesic-described saline increases tourniquet pain-tolerance duration; the corresponding naloxone condition does not retain that increase. Hidden naloxone alone gives no significant baseline change. This supports an opioid-receptor contribution to the tested response without directly measuring opioid release or proving a single universal placebo mechanism.");
operating("placebo-expectation-intervention", "placebo-opioid-sensitive-tolerance", "amanzio1999-expectation-antagonism", "Framing/injection conditions and opioid antagonism change the measured tolerance outcome within this experimental pain paradigm.", "Existing human participants in the specified ischemic arm-pain procedure", "Analgesic-described open saline or naloxone, with no-treatment and hidden-naloxone controls", "Participant-controlled duration until pain is intolerable", "This is a composite context/pharmacology intervention, not a pure intervention on an abstract belief or direct proof of every step in a descending neural pathway.");
study("amanzio1999-conditioning-specificity", "amanzio1999-placebo-dissection", "Human ischemic arm pain after morphine or ketorolac conditioning",
  "Pair analgesic-described morphine or ketorolac with the pain test on two days. On the test day substitute saline or naloxone and describe the injection either as the earlier analgesic or as a neutral antibiotic. Keep eight conditioning groups, their prior drugs, verbal cues and pre/post untreated days separate.",
  "Pain tolerance under saline/naloxone substitution after distinct drug histories", "Naloxone sensitivity differs with conditioning drug and test instructions.",
  ["An antibiotic description reduces explicit analgesic cues but does not demonstrate absence of all conscious expectation.", "The nonopioid arm does not identify a unique alternative transmitter or receptor; naloxone-insensitivity is not proof that no endogenous process changes.", "Conditioning without expectation cues still follows earlier active medication; the test-day saline condition is not a drug-naive history."], [0, 2, 3]);
observation("placebo-conditioning-input", "Drug history and test-framing factorial distinctions", "amanzio1999-conditioning-specificity", "The source contrasts morphine versus ketorolac preconditioning, analgesic versus antibiotic test descriptions and saline versus naloxone substitution. These are distinct group-level interventions rather than one pooled expectancy dose.");
observation("placebo-conditioning-opioid-dissociation", "Conditioning-dependent antagonist sensitivity", "amanzio1999-conditioning-specificity", "Morphine-conditioned tolerance responses are naloxone-sensitive under either tested description. Ketorolac-conditioned responses with analgesic cues are partly naloxone-sensitive; under antibiotic description the source reports a residual naloxone-insensitive response. The ketorolac difference between descriptions is not conventionally significant in the reported comparison, so no measured additive expectation effect is inferred there.");
operating("placebo-conditioning-input", "placebo-conditioning-opioid-dissociation", "amanzio1999-conditioning-specificity", "The conditioning and framing history changes which tested tolerance response survives opioid antagonism.", "Previously conditioned human participants in the source pain protocol", "Specified previous drug, test-day framing and saline/naloxone substitution", "Pain-tolerance duration and antagonist sensitivity", "No universal conscious-versus-unconscious partition, novel receptor identity, patient cure or common circuit threshold is established.");

